{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/pulls/368","id":4718953860,"node_id":"PR_kwDOTfIvP88AAAABGUWFhA","html_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/pull/368","diff_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/pull/368.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel/Math/pull/368.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/issues/368","number":368,"state":"open","locked":false,"title":"30004320: reviewed Laurent descent partials; original unsolved 5/5","user":{"login":"AlecKriebel","id":6109370,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjYxMDkzNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/6109370?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel","html_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Accept scoped partial findings for Laurent-series homogeneous-space descent (30004320 / OWR-17295-004). The original arbitrary-field question remains **unsolved, 5/5**: no universal proof or admissible constant homogeneous-space counterexample is established.\n\nEvery positive theorem retains a smooth affine acting group and smooth schematic/fppf homogeneous variety. The proven classes are finite tame stabilizers; arbitrary finite étale stabilizers when H1(k,G)=1; all torus and multiplicative-type stabilizers, including nonsmooth ones; and smooth stabilizers with reductive identity and prime-to-characteristic Weyl/component orders. Full prescribed gerbe objects and their underlying torsors are preserved where necessary. Orbit matching is over the full parameter-root union where stated. Wild Artin–Schreier, mu_p, wound-unipotent and PGL_p examples constrain auxiliary proof methods; none is an original-problem counterexample.\n\nFlorence's homogeneous-space theorem for all perfect fields and Florence–Gille's constant-affine-group torsor theorem over arbitrary fields are credited prior work. Structural ramification, gerbe, centralizer, rigidification and maximal-torus inputs retain their exact hypotheses. AOV's corrigendum was checked. Historical partial-result novelty and current global openness are not certified; old exhaustive branch/ref-search claims were not independently reenacted.\n\nRoot and three independent adversarial families sealed source-first and mathematical reconstructions before checker outputs, final status, history and previous review. Supplementary source readings and limitations are recorded in their actual chronology. Root validates all54 original Git/API paths/modes, all53 target files, eight manifests/130 bindings,42 real historical author bindings, five actual parent-linked checkpoints and eleven freshly obtained primary PDF identities. Full stored outputs reproduce 128,694 author assertions and8,664 historical controls. New families add13,339 separately reproduced finite mathematical controls and14 drift negatives. These computations support the written proofs and do not certify universal descent by finite testing.\n\nRemaining gaps include arbitrary wild noncommutative/unipotent stabilizers, wild Weyl/component orders, and broader nonsmooth acting-group scope. Fresh current-head/body/base/tree and whole-package checks must pass before merge; actual parents, files and full queue are verified afterward. Only this problem's queue status/turn cells change against current main, preserving every other queue byte and all original mathematical files.\n\nOpenAI tools were used extensively in research, drafting and adversarial verification. This is an unrefereed AI-assisted partial packet; no human peer review is claimed. Accept as partial work with the original problem unresolved. No paper, Zenodo upload, DOI, tracker row or release accompanies this acceptance.\n","created_at":"2026-10-02T16:38:18Z","updated_at":"2026-10-03T13:32:17Z","closed_at":null,"merged_at":null,"merge_commit_sha":"8fe23808ca87a822ddf24f551092be3bdf68a13f","assignees":[],"requested_reviewers":[],"requested_teams":[],"labels":[],"milestone":null,"draft":false,"commits_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/pulls/368/commits","review_comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/pulls/368/comments","review_comment_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/pulls/comments{/number}","comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/issues/368/comments","statuses_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/AlecKriebel/Math/statuses/e8a53a05b309bda2a7d60136d8fb5a4e21e313fd","head":{"label":"AlecKriebel:math/30004320-laurent-descent-reviewed","ref":"math/30004320-laurent-descent-reviewed","sha":"e8a53a05b309bda2a7d60136d8fb5a4e21e313fd","user":{"login":"AlecKriebel","id":6109370,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjYxMDkzNzA=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/6109370?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel","html_url":"https://github.com/AlecKriebel","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/AlecKriebel